<<~ kanawai "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/ref/RFC-2119">>



! The Membership Doctrine ~ track contracts, never identities

> The privacy keel of the whole mesh, held from a one-operator house to planetary
> public infrastructure. The line it draws decides whether the DreamNet becomes a
> commons or a panopticon. Operator-ruled 2026-07-21.

<<~ ahu #/the-invariant>>

!! The invariant

> ''The Nexus tracks operator CONTRACTS, never user IDENTITIES. The only persistent trace a human
> leaves is (a) a carriage contract they consciously signed, or (b) a public Handle-card they
> consciously posted to `crossroads`. Everything else — who used the protocols, when, what they
> read, their private data — leaves no central record.''

!!! ★ BELONGING CARRIES TWO CONDITIONS — the contract, and the grammar ★

''An operator belongs to a Nexus by CONTRACT and by AGREEING WITH THE GRAMMAR VECTORS traded there'' (operator-ruled). The carriage board records the first; the second never becomes a row, and that reads as the point rather than a gap — ''the invariant above still holds whole''. A node that cannot read what a Nexus trades cannot carry it faithfully, cannot verify what it relays, and nothing it never parsed can bind it: //contract without grammar-agreement reads as a signature over an unread page//.

''THE SECOND CONDITION RIDES A KEY, NEVER AN AUDIT, and so it never enters a registry.'' The field's one surviving instance (MLS, RFC 9420) folds the declared grammar into the key schedule — ''disagree on the grammar and you derive a different key and fail to decrypt''. It never ASKS a peer whether it understands, because you cannot detect a peer that lies about understanding; it makes the lie non-computable. Twenty-four years of the alternative stand recorded, the same declared-critical-and-not-processed bug shipping in 2002, 2011 and 2026 across three vendors.

''So the check adds no trace, no roster row, and no new authority'' — it rides where the crypto already rides, bilaterally, peer-local, exactly like the rest of this doctrine.

''AND ITS SCALE STAYS BOUNDED, which the no-roster ruling already anticipated.'' The excluder's market share bounds exclusion power: three fields built this gate correctly and three retreated under open-internet pressure. The one that held gates at the ''group'' boundary, where the members make up the whole relevant population and the membership stays local. ''Deployable at Lararium and Nexus scale; at DreamNet scale it predicts the same retreat'' — which explains why a bilateral leased contract per operator, and never a confederation-wide roster, makes the shape that survives. <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/grammar-invariants">>

— the condition stands ruled; ''its mechanism stands UNBUILT'', and a prior treatment waits on another worldline to surface from the sensorium.

The corpo's first act stands up the user table — the row per
human that becomes the product. This doctrine ''chooses to not have the table.'' A Nexus runs
public infrastructure at any scale and still answers "who are your users?" with the only honest
answer a sovereign commons holds: ''we do not hold that — ask them.'' This enacts the anti-SIN
stance (#carry-contract, #the-frame) in the one place it would otherwise erode first.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-two-stacks>>

!! The two cap-stacks ~ operator contracts, user soft-attaches

A human's posture rides the vessels in their PersonaGroup pool — a ''posture, not a caste'', chosen
vessel by vessel (#/identity-classes):

<<~ has Stack OPERATOR "runs/a node vessel does/CONTRACTS with the Nexus (owes MANDATORY carriage) trace/a wax-sealed carriage contract — consensual, minimal, revocable (Kapae)">>
<<~ has Stack USER "runs/browser-style vessels, no infra does/WALKS on an invite from any member face (boot-seeded from a node on the rhizome, own cap-stack, reads/writes through blind relays) trace/NONE — no roster, no account, no user table, no quorum admit">>

''An operator contracts because carriage carries a mutual obligation the mesh must check'' — a Nexus has
to know its infrastructure-peers to gossip the antigen and split the carry. ''A user owes nothing,
carries nothing, so belongs in no roster'' — the node that boot-seeds a user holds no membership
entry for it. A user turns operator by adding a node vessel and contracting; the stack shifts with
the pool.

!!! The faces ~ four cap stacks that compose on a persona

''A face names a cap stack, and four stand'':

| face | caps |
|---|---|
| ''end user'' | `walk` — //an operator KEEPS a hearth; a user WALKS the road// |
| ''Lamplighter'' | `carry` · `tend/island` · `tend/reach` (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/lamplighters#/reach">>) |
| ''Kahu'' | the Nexus-scale `quorum-share`; the engine cadence rides a separate threshold, and the corpus-kahu curate at DreamNet scale (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/kahu#/nexus-scale">>) |
| ''Guardian'' | a recovery key over a persona's KEL — Guardians recover the PERSON, and carry weight in a Nexus KEL only inside its opt-in catastrophe rite; one human may hold it beside a Kahu seat, in a separate custody (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/kahu#/nexus-scale">>) |

''Founding names an ACT, never a face'': it seats the first kahu quorum, and the founder then holds
a Kahu seat in that Nexus.

''Cap stacks COMPOSE on a persona — combined by default, split only by the holder's own act.'' A solo
operator holds one persona for all their local infrastructure, and every cap they hold rides it; no
face model makes a persona split the price of a second cap stack. ''At first contract-in with other
humans the system OFFERS a split and never forces one'', since at that contract-in correlation
across faces first starts to cost. Personas multiply for the holder's own purposes, most of them
nothing to do with mesh upkeep.

''The target scale model is the SMALL CLAN'': a family of several operators and several users, each
holding several personas. The design scales up from the clan, never down from the civic.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-three-admissions>>

!! Three admissions ~ walking, contracting and seating never share a name

''Three relations bring a human into a Nexus, and one name for all three blurs the act each one enacts.''
The criterion that keeps them apart reads the same as everywhere else in the house: different ceremony,
different signer, different trace.

|! relation |! who acts |! what it takes |! what it leaves |
| a USER ''walks'' | a hearth that hosts in the Nexus, and any walker it hosts | an invite, redeemed once at the host's own gate for a keyed grant (#/the-invite) | nothing: no quorum, no board entry, no who-invited-whom — the host keeps random nonces and a count |
| an operator or place ''contracts'' | the subject, and ≥k of the kahu quorum | the presented admit, the subject's consent, ≥k Kahu countersigning, the carriage board, the Lamplighter office (`carry` · `tend`) | the carriage contract — and nothing more (#/the-operator-contract) |
| a member is ''seated'' | the kahu quorum, through the seal lineage | a seat in the lineage's per-seat commitment | the seal lineage itself (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/kahu#/nexus-scale">>) |

''Walking reads as HOSPITALITY, never admission.'' An invite hands a guest the road; it confers no
standing, asks no quorum and leaves no trace on any board (#/the-invite). ''Contracting reads as the one
ADMISSION'' — the word //admit// names this relation and no other, and the admit travels with its subject
(#/the-operator-contract). ''Seating reads as SUCCESSION'' — a member joins the quorum that admits, and
the seal lineage records it as keys and a threshold, never as a roster.

The cadence threshold rides none of the three as a base relation: it seats operators who already
contract, by its own lineage (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/kahu#/contract">>).

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-operator-contract>>

!! The operator contract ~ the minimal wax-sealed payload

An operator's membership entry carries the ''floor and nothing above it'' (operator-ruled):

<<~ scale payload "operator-pubkey -> accepts-carriage -> charter-epoch">>

''No name, no email, no device list, no behavior.'' A quorum-signed `MembershipEntry` (the antigen's
allow-twin, `blocked{}` ⊥ `members{}`, #carry-contract) — the operator signs "I accept carriage",
≥k kahu counter-sign it against the charter epoch; per-Nexus, causal-island-local, read as-of-last-
sync (no global now, #causal-islands), revocable by Kapae. ''This marks the maximum the system ever
holds about a contracting operator.''

''The admit travels with its subject.'' The subject PRESENTS its quorum-signed entry at the wire, and
the gate reads it against the deny-only `blocked{}` — never `members{}` folded into an allow roster
(<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/kahu#/nexus-scale">>, which also carries what the code
holds, the cut-over it owes, the subject stamp's nexus-scoped leaf, and the gate's shape).

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/two-maps>>

!! Two maps ~ the per-Nexus LEAF and the persona ROOT never link

''No proof binding a per-Nexus LEAF to a persona ROOT ever travels on the wire'' (operator-ruled). A
leaf exists so that one human admitted to two Nexuses reads as two unrelated subjects; a single signed
edge from root to leaf, presented once, undoes that for every Nexus that ever sees it.

The law, in five clauses:

# A socket that presents a leaf admit MUST NOT present a root-signed edge. ''One socket, one face.''
# The ''carriage membership consult'' reads the LEAF map alone. A peer enters that map only from a
  presented admit the verifier reads HELD against its own Nexus — the deny board and the antigen
  standing apart — together with the leaf's own proof of possession over THIS socket's challenge nonce.
  A folded board, a raw wire key, and a root edge each fill nothing.
# The ''realm consult'' reads a separate ROOT map, filled only by a root edge proven at the seat that
  reads it.
# The two maps fill INDEPENDENTLY and never link. No reader joins them, no consult falls through from
  one to the other, and a leaf MUST NOT answer a root question (nor a root a leaf question). Where a seat
  holds no proven root edge, the root map answers null for that peer — an honest nothing, never a leaf
  promoted to stand in.
# ''One face per vessel per Nexus, on every socket.'' A vessel that presents a leaf to a Nexus — or will
  present one — presents NO root-signed edge to that Nexus on ANY socket, before the admit or after it.
  Every socket a vessel opens proves the same vessel key, so a root edge on one socket and a leaf admit on
  another link root to leaf through that key as surely as both on one socket would. The wire therefore
  carries no contract slot at all: a self-founded vessel with no admit for the dialed Nexus presents its
  ContactCard alone, and only the fleet edge (signed by a root the vessel does NOT hold) rides beside a card.

The residual correlation, named: the vessel key spans every Nexus the vessel dials as ONE key, so two
Nexuses that compare the wire keys they proved can still learn that one vessel stands in both — though
never which root, nor which leaves. A per-Nexus wire key closes that, owed to the re-found.

''ROOT ⊥ LEAF stands as its own pair that must not fuse'', beside members ⊥ carriers and never folded
into it. Carriage asks //may this face carry for this Nexus//; the realm asks //who stands behind this
face//. A wire that answers both at once builds the correlator this doctrine exists to refuse. The
subject stamp's nexus-scoped leaf and the gate's shape stand at
<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/kahu#/nexus-scale">>.

!!! Prior art ~ two vindicate, one warns

* ''ACDC'' issues each credential to a unique issuee AID, so two credentials held by one controller do
  not correlate on their subject; linking stays the holder's own later act, never the wire's.
* ''SPIFFE/SPIRE'' attests a node and a workload as two identities by two attestations; a workload SVID
  carries no signature from the node identity, and no verifier needs one.
* ''Matrix cross-signing'' stands as the cautionary case: the master key signs the self-signing key,
  which signs every device, and those signatures publish — so every device a user holds links to one
  master identity for anyone who reads the server. The leaf law refuses exactly that publication.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-invite>>

!! The invite ~ a hosted grant the guest carries, burned by its host, about no one

''HOSPITALITY RIDES A HEARTH'' (operator-ruled). A hearth that carries a Nexus HOSTS walkers in it. The
hearth publishes a ''hosting act'' on its own HOSTING DOC in the Nexus, one doc per (Nexus, hearth gate
key) (`hosting-act`): its own per-Nexus leaf signs the Nexus, its hosting public key, its hand-out cap and
the act before it. The act's CID names the EPOCH. No hearth picks a random epoch id, because a fresh id per
walker would tag each one. A walker computes the doc from what its invite names — the Nexus and the gate key
it pins — so every walker of one hearth reads the one doc, and the Nexus's carriers replicate it for the
cross-check. The act grants nothing and names only the hearth. One key per act derives from the leaf seed
and the act's link (`hosting-key`), with no counter.

''THE WORN FACE HOSTS'' (operator-ruled). The per-Nexus leaf of the face the vessel WEARS signs the act — the same face its walks present, one
face per vessel per Nexus. No roster order picks anything, and a vessel wearing no face hosts no one. ''ROOT-ON-FOUNDER'' keeps a face on one hosting store, and
the first roll guards it: before its first act in a Nexus, a roll reads the hosting doc, and an act this face
signed there that no local state holds refuses the roll aloud and writes nothing. A face hosting from two
places surfaces; nothing detects it beyond that read.

''THE CARRIAGE BOARD KEEPS THE CARRIERS' LEDGER.'' The carriage board stands as the ALLOW-twin of the antigen: the
carriers' contracts, their charter epoch, the quorum's signed admits and revokes, and the roll anchors — self-published
infrastructure acts, never a guest. Its readership: the relation that holds contracts: the carriers, the
kahu and the quorum. A walker never reads it, so the least-trusted relation never sees the Nexus's
infrastructure graph; compartments outrank admission control.

''THE INVITE RIDES A TOKEN, THE STANDING A GRANT, BOTH KEYED.'' An invite carries a POPRF token
(RFC 9497, ristretto255; Privacy-Pass-shaped, not RFC 9578 wire-conformant) under the epoch's key and a
PURPOSE label (`host-invite` or `walker-invite`), carried as one `lar-invite:` string with the hearth's
gate key and Nexus. It names no guest, and its nonce runs random. The newcomer presents it AT THE HOST'S OWN
GATE, inside the authenticated handshake, together with a CLAIM its own leaf seed derives from the nonce.
The host burns the nonce in its per-epoch spent-set (`hosting-spend`): random nonces and claim digests,
about no one, fsynced before the verdict. Each digest binds the claim to the leaf that proved over the
redeeming socket. The same claim under the same leaf reads as a retry and earns the identical grant
(refuse-before-destroy). A different claim, or the same claim under another leaf, meets silence, and the
burn reads before it writes, so a captured `{token, claim}` replayed under a thief's leaf destroys nothing.
The host then pushes a GRANT (`hosting-grant`): a keyed PRF tag over the Nexus, the walker's leaf, the
epoch, a LINEAGE derived from nonce and claim, and how many rolls it survived. ''Only the host verifies
it'', so a seized phone proves nothing to anyone else. The guest keeps its invite until a dial carrying the
grant stands, then presents the grant on every dial.

''TWO LIVE EPOCHS, NO EXCHANGE.'' Tokens and grants hold at the current and the previous epoch. The host
renews a grant presented at the previous epoch and pushes it again. A ''hard roll'' rolls twice: every
spent-set and every grant outside the two live epochs dies with no list to walk.

''THE WALLET FILLS AT ONCE, AND IT VESTS'' (operator-ruled). A walker on a grant mints its whole allowance
for the epoch in ONE blind batch, never one invite on demand, because mint time would link. A HOST-minted
invite grants its guest floor 1 at once. A WALKER-minted invite gives a budget that vests only at the
host's NEXT roll, so a chain grows at most one hop per roll. The purpose label separates the two, and it
never identifies the minting walker. One burn marker per lineage per epoch keeps the digest of the batch,
so a retry finds its marker and burns nothing new. Every fill drops the wallet's tokens from any epoch the
host does not hold live, and a hand-out takes the newest, so a walker never hands out a dead invite.

''NO ONE CAN COMPUTE WHO INVITED WHOM.'' The blinded batch stays unlinkable to the tokens it becomes, so the
hearth learns that some walker it hosts minted some number of invites this epoch, and never whose invite
a newcomer redeemed. The operator sees a redemption COUNT per epoch: a number, never a row. The CLI keeps the
three hosting verbs: `lares host` (the state and the count), `lares host roll`, `lares host invite`. While
the vessel stands, each runs inside it, against the replica its walkers sync, so a roll reaches them with
no restart; with no vessel standing, the CLI runs the same door and owns the store for that moment.

''A NODE MAY WALK'' (operator-ruled). The sorter sorts by PROVEN RELATION, never by device type, so a node
vessel holding a grant sorts as a walker exactly like a browser leaf. Walking grants it no carry duty and
leaves its own private Nexus untouched. A walker stands as a person, so it walks as the face its vessel WEARS:
no roster order picks anything, and a vessel wearing no face walks nowhere. A node keeps its walk with
three verbs: `lares walk` (where it walks, its standing there, its wallet's count), `lares walk take`
(keep a carried invite; the vessel's hearth dial walks in on it) and `lares walk invite` (hand out the
newest invite). A node carries none of its own documents at a hearth: it serves as its own durable store.
''ONE HEARTH, OWED WIDER.'' A vessel holds one hearth dial, so it walks at one hearth: a take for a second
hearth, beside a walk or a join dial that already stands, refuses aloud and writes nothing. Walking at
several hearths at once waits on one dial per (hearth, Nexus).

''ONE HOP, BURNED, REMEMBERED BY NO ONE'': the host's spent-set closes the invite, no wall clock bounds it,
the inviter carries no liability for the guest, and no record anywhere — board, host or newcomer — chains
who invited whom, because that chain would read as a roster. Membership never confers trust;
compartments outrank admission control.

''TWO LIMITS, NAMED.'' The ''cabal-of-one equivocation bound'': in a one-hearth Nexus the hearth relays its
own hosting doc, so nothing detects a forked hosting act until a second carrier holds the doc. The
''social-inference limit'': in a small household the count, the timing and who is in the room can tell the
host whose invite came in. The cryptography removes the record. It cannot remove what the host can see.

''WHAT WAITS FOR THE RE-FOUND.'' A per-Nexus wire key (one gate key serves every Nexus a hearth
carries, so a gate's posture folds over all of them); a sealed custody root (the hosting key derives from a
leaf seed that rests cleartext on disk); full Noise IK (the transport and the knock path still leak to a
passive watcher).

''The reputation-cost braid'' — the idea that an invite rides on the inviter's reputation as a cost
(Sybil-resistance, accountability) — reserves to the ''Cabal tier'', never the general invite: a
cabal is a bilateral verb two operators actively DO (#/cabal-realm), and a reputation-priced JOIN to a
realm lives THERE, where the graph already stands mutual + consented, not on the traceless general invite.
''The reservation runs as TWO DOMAINS, never one dial.'' `hosting-token` carries the hearth's POPRF
evaluation for one Nexus and epoch, names no voucher, writes no edge, and burns at its host — the general
invite. `cabal-invite`
(`lar-cabal-invite/v1`) names its `voucherDid` in the clear and feeds the vouch-DAG the lineage price
walks. ''They never collapse'': merging them destroys the tracelessness on purpose-built ground. The
general invite forgoes precisely what the Cabal tier requires — an attributable voucher to
carry the cost (`admission-on-a-lineage#the-standing`).

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-walkers-carriage>>

!! The walker's carriage ~ carried sealed, reclaimed by the guest's own rhythm, never a backup

''THE HEARTH CARRIES A WALKER'S OWN DOCUMENTS AS SEALED CIPHERTEXT — CARRY ⊥ READ'' (operator-ruled). The
walker seals each document under a secret its own per-Nexus leaf derives (`walk-carry-seal`) and hands the
hearth only ciphertext and its content address. The hearth verifies the address, which needs no key,
stores the bytes, and can never open them or confirm a guessed plaintext. The read-cap stays in the
walker's own receipt.

''ONE RECORD PER LEAF, REACHED ONLY BY A GRANT THAT PROVES THAT LEAF.'' The hearth files a guest's
carriage under a key it derives from the guest's PROVEN per-Nexus leaf under its own leaf seed
(`hosting-carry`). Every grant names that leaf and every socket's leaf proof proves it, so proof alone
reaches the record and nothing the hearth hands out names it. A grant lapsed past two rolls dies, and
a guest that walks back in on a new invite under the same leaf reaches its carriage again. A fresh leaf opens
a fresh carriage, and a kapae'd leaf reaches nothing through a new one: kapae binds the leaf. Every dial on
the leaf counts as a CONTACT, on the grant arm and the token arm alike, so the notice reaches a guest by a plain
dial. A record holds SCALARS and the blobs: the bytes it carries, the hearth epoch it last saw the guest,
the guest's own typical gap between contacts, and a pending mark. It never holds a lineage, a leaf or a
grant tag. The guest keeps its own receipts. The quota bounds each guest's carriage, and the hearth refuses a deposit past it.

''TWO BOUNDS, NAMED.'' The records form a directory of opaque rows, so a seizer of the hearth's store reads
how many guests it carries for and each row's contact rhythm — a count and a cadence, never who. A holder
of the hearth's leaf seed can test a KNOWN leaf against the rows; that adds no linkage the hearth lacks,
since it already sees that leaf prove. Both bounds stand until the sealed custody root closes the store at
rest.

''RECLAIM RUNS PONO — PRESSURE THE ONLY TRIGGER, THE GUEST'S OWN RHYTHM THE MEASURE, NOTICE BEFORE DISCARD.''
* Lapsing alone evicts nothing. Only a deposit that would pass the hearth's total triggers a
reclaim.
* A reclaim reads each guest's lapse as a RATIO against its OWN typical gap, both counted in the hearth's
own epochs, so the hearth's cadence cancels. A hearth rolling ten times as often reads the same
eligibility, and no flat count of epochs decides.
* The guest most lapsed for itself goes first, and the reclaim takes only enough to cover the shortfall.
It never takes a guest within its own rhythm while a long-lapsed one stands.
* The reclaim first MARKS a taken guest pending. Its next contact hears the notice (`hosting/notice`), and a
contact in a later epoch clears the mark. The hearth discards its bytes only once the mark has stood unrenewed
through the guest's own threshold again.

''THE PERSONAGROUP HOLDS THE DURABLE COPY.'' A hearth's carriage offers a convenience a walker reaches from any
dial. The walker's own devices hold its documents. No surface may present carriage as backup.

''DILIGENCE ≠ TITLE, ANSWERED.'' The reclaim never takes a guest that keeps its own rhythm while a lapsed one stands,
but its presence buys no tenure. The quota bounds what any grant's diligence can hold. The hearth's own
roll ends every grant it stops renewing, and a hard roll ends them all with no list to walk. Presence earns
the guest NOTICE before the hearth takes its space back, never title to the hearth's disk
(<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/waking-floor">> · <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/cabal-realm#/the-unswept-corner">>).

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-carried-cap>>

!! What a face CARRIES ~ a capability, never a standing

''INVERSION OF CONTROL, at the verification surface.'' A platform holds the roster and answers //is this one a member//, which explains why the centre repays capture. Inverted: ''the face carries the credential, the group holds no list, and verification runs OFFLINE against a published key''. Nobody stands home to ask, so asking seizes nothing.

|! |! a CAP |! a STANDING |
| answers | what may I do | what do I count as |
| flows | DOWN, by delegation | UP, by conferral |
| form | keyed (verified by its issuer alone) or signed · scoped · epoch-bound | ''a live query, never a stored number'' |
| carried on a device | ''yes'' | ''never'' |

''That split makes a phone shelter rather than liability.'' Seizing it yields ONE capability — not a roster, and no standing, because standing never rode the device. It also closes the laundering seam: a captured key inherits no legitimacy, since legitimacy never sat on the object.

''ACTING AS THE GROUP runs by attenuated delegation'' rooted in the realm's own sentinel agent — a self-certifying root with no apex above it. The delegation says //may speak for this pilina about X, until epoch N//, ''never //is this pilina//''; an unattenuated group delegation rebuilds the apex inside the group.

''THE GROUP OFFERING VERIFICATION — and the mode this stack REFUSES.'' An online //is this one ours// endpoint needs reachability (dead in a cut-off mesh), logs every presentation, and ''rebuilds the roster on the group's side''. What stands instead: offline verification against the published seal, and for anything richer an ''un-thresholded attestor bundle'' where members sign independently, the count crosses, and the sufficiency rule stays structurally absent.

> ''AND THE GROUP NEVER VERIFIES A HUMAN.'' It attests a RELATION — //this face holds a relation with us// — and what that relation means in the world stays the group's own rite, un-modelled. ''That refusal enacts the un-confiscatability'': a state can void a card because a card claims to name a person and its issuer can un-name them. Nothing here ever claimed to name a person, so no claim stands to void. Seize the device and the relation survives to be re-attested; seize a state card and the bearer reads as nobody.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-user-scope>>

!! The user scope ~ soft-attach + inversion-of-control social graphs

A user vessel-pool ''boot-seeds from a node on the rhizome'', receives its own cap-stack, and reads
the public planes + its own private data ''through that node's blind relay'' (transit ⊥ read,
#identity-classes). It signs no carriage contract, so it leaves no roster trace.

''Users build their OWN social graphs — inversion of control.'' The user owns the graph (its own
handle-book of others' nyms, #persona-policy), never the Nexus. The Nexus federates only the public
Handle-cards a user consciously posts to `crossroads`; the connections a user draws between them stay
the user's own, off any central plane. The Kowloon-submodule pattern
carries this (partially wired, polish deferred — a 2a build).

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-publishing-surface>>

!! The publishing surface ~ four acts under one word, and a ladder

''//Publish// packed four distinct acts, and only one of them may never happen'':

|! act |! what it exposes |! may we |
| the DECLARATION | that a partition stands, signed | yes — at a chosen tier |
| the METHOD | how a reading gets computed | yes — it rides as code |
| the READING | what a reading returned | yes |
| the INPUTS | ''who the members are'' | NEVER |

Fields that manufacture standing-to-object publish all four. ''This stack publishes three and withholds the fourth'' — and that suffices, because rival readings that disagree stay checkable ''without anyone holding the member list''.

''The surface already stands, tiered.'' The @crossroads / @nexus boards ride the always-carried MANDATORY tier every contracted operator carries free; a realm's charter rides veil-public; below that, content wraps in a Handle's, a persona's, or a dyad's own crypto. ''`carry ⊥ read ⊥ contract` holds at the transport'' and reads as model law rather than a transport detail.

> ''STANDING-TO-OBJECT SCOPES TO THE TIER AT WHICH A THING GETS PUBLISHED.''

A realm publishing its declaration at the public tier gains outside challengers; one publishing at Handle tier keeps its challengers internal. ''Same machinery, one dial'' — and a realm under threat withdraws the tier without losing the faculty. Note what this does NOT buy: it names a procedural capacity to challenge, never an accrued position. ''Standing-to-object ⊥ `kūlana``'' (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/identity-enacts-relation">>).

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-transition>>

!! From the house to public infrastructure ~ lock it in at the floor

The doctrine holds identically at every scale — a one-operator house, an invite-only alpha, a
planetary public Nexus. ''Lock the minimalism in at the floor'': a table left soft grows the first time
someone asks for "just a little telemetry." Built as the floor, it stays the floor — the mesh
''cannot'' surveil its users because it never stood up the surface to hold them.

<<~/ahu>>

